-- What Is WordPress?
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Say what WordPress is and what the two versions actually differ on', 'Understand how themes, plugins and content fit together', 'Judge when a website is not the right answer at all'],
  notes = 'WORDPRESS IS SOFTWARE THAT RUNS ON A SERVER AND LETS SOMEBODY WITHOUT CODE EDIT A WEBSITE. It powers a very large share of the web, which matters for a practical reason: there is an answer to almost every question you will have, and an employer or client is likely to already be using it.

THE FIRST THING TO GET STRAIGHT, BECAUSE THE TWO ARE CONSTANTLY CONFUSED. WordPress.ORG is the free software you install on hosting you control; you can install any plugin, any theme, and you own everything. WordPress.COM is a hosted service run by a company, easier to start and restricted on the free and cheap plans — plugins and full control usually arrive only on the more expensive tiers. THIS COURSE, AND ALMOST ALL PAID CLIENT WORK, MEANS THE .ORG VERSION. If somebody tells you plugins cannot be installed, they are on .com.

THE THREE PARTS, AND KEEPING THEM SEPARATE IS THE WHOLE MENTAL MODEL. CONTENT — your pages, posts and images — lives in a database and belongs to you. The THEME controls how it looks. PLUGINS add functions the software does not have. Because they are separate, you can change the theme without losing content, and that is the single most reassuring fact for a nervous beginner.

PAGES VERSUS POSTS: pages are permanent and standalone — about, contact, services. Posts are dated entries in a stream. Most small business sites need five pages and no posts at all, and starting a blog nobody will maintain is a common waste.

WHAT IT IS GOOD AT: a site somebody non-technical must update themselves, content that grows, a shop, anything needing a specific function that a plugin already provides.

AND NOW THE HONEST PART THAT MOST COURSES SKIP: SOMETIMES A WEBSITE IS NOT THE ANSWER. A business whose customers all arrive through WhatsApp and Facebook, that sells ten products, may be better served by a complete Google Business Profile, a good Facebook page and a WhatsApp catalogue — all free, all where the customers already are. A site nobody visits, with hosting renewing every year, is a cost rather than an asset. The right question is always where the customers already look, not whether a website would be nice to have.

WHERE A SITE DOES EARN ITS KEEP HERE: being findable on a map search, looking established to a corporate or NGO client who will check before paying, taking bookings or orders without a conversation, and owning a place on the internet that no platform can take away from you.',
  practice_activity = 'Find three real small businesses you know and answer one question for each: where do their customers actually find them now? Then decide, in a sentence each, whether a website would add something a Google Business Profile and a Facebook page would not. Being able to give an honest no is what makes your yes worth paying for. Then open any WordPress site and identify which parts of what you see are content, which are theme, and which are probably a plugin.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the practical difference between WordPress.org and WordPress.com?", "options": [".org is the free software on hosting you control, with any plugin or theme; .com is a hosted service that restricts plugins on cheaper plans", ".org is for developers and .com is for businesses", ".com is the newer version that replaced .org", "They are the same software with different websites"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "When might a website NOT be the right answer for a small business?", "options": ["When its customers all arrive through WhatsApp and Facebook, where a business profile and catalogue may serve better for free", "When the business has fewer than ten employees", "When the owner cannot write code", "When the business already has a Facebook page"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '036a71ba-bb5c-49d2-94d2-b8e5a32a1c54';

-- Choosing Hosting
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Tell a domain apart from hosting and know why it matters', 'Compare hosts on the things that actually bite later', 'Register the domain so the client owns it'],
  notes = 'TWO SEPARATE THINGS THAT BEGINNERS MERGE. The DOMAIN is the address people type; you rent it yearly from a registrar. HOSTING is the computer your site lives on; you rent that too. They can come from the same company or different ones, and knowing they are separate is what lets you move one without losing the other.

NOW THE MOST IMPORTANT PARAGRAPH IN THIS LESSON, AND IT IS NOT TECHNICAL. THE DOMAIN MUST BE REGISTERED IN THE CLIENT''S OWN NAME, WITH THEIR OWN EMAIL, ON AN ACCOUNT THEY CONTROL. A developer who registers it in their own name — even helpfully, even with the best intentions — is holding the business hostage, and the day that relationship sours the client loses the address their customers know. Set it up correctly at the start, give them the login, and say plainly that they own it. Doing this is one of the clearest signals that you are a professional rather than somebody who learned WordPress last month, and it will win you work.

WHAT ACTUALLY MATTERS WHEN COMPARING HOSTS, roughly in order. UPTIME AND SPEED for visitors in the region you serve. SUPPORT you can reach and that answers — test it with a question before you buy. BACKUPS included and, more importantly, RESTORABLE; a backup you have never restored is a hope. FREE SSL, which is now standard and should not be an upsell. And ROOM TO GROW without a painful migration.

THE RENEWAL PRICE IS THE REAL PRICE. Introductory offers are routinely a fraction of what year two costs, and the difference is often several times the first bill. Find the renewal price before you buy and budget against that, for yourself and for any client.

LOCAL HOSTING DESERVES A REAL LOOK RATHER THAN A REFLEX. Kenyan and regional providers can be paid by M-Pesa, which removes the card problem entirely; they support local domain extensions properly; their support is in your time zone; and for visitors who are mostly here, a server closer by is genuinely faster. International hosts may offer more features and cheaper introductory pricing but usually require a card and give support at inconvenient hours. Weigh it for the specific client rather than assuming either answer.

SHARED HOSTING IS CORRECT FOR ALMOST EVERY FIRST SITE. Managed and dedicated hosting solve problems you do not have yet, at prices you do not need to pay yet.

AND KEEP THE CREDENTIALS SOMEWHERE SAFE AND SEPARATE — registrar, host, WordPress admin. Losing access to a live client site is a genuinely serious professional failure, and it is prevented by ten minutes of organisation at the start.',
  practice_activity = 'Compare three hosts — at least one local and one international — on the things listed here, and write the numbers down side by side: renewal price rather than the offer, whether backups are included, whether SSL is free, what payment methods they take, and how fast support answers a real question you send them. Then write the sentence you will use with a client to explain that the domain will be registered in their name, on their account, and that they own it.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Whose name should a client''s domain be registered in?", "options": ["The client''s own name, email and account — a developer holding it controls the business''s address", "The developer''s, so it can be managed easily", "Either, as long as it is documented", "The hosting company''s, which simplifies renewal"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Which price should you budget against when choosing a host?", "options": ["The renewal price, which is often several times the introductory offer", "The introductory offer, which is what you actually pay first", "The monthly price before tax", "The price of the next tier up, for room to grow"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '741403f1-bb98-4512-90e1-680f0828411e';

-- Using the Block Editor
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build a page out of blocks and reuse what you have built', 'Design for the phone first, because that is what visitors use', 'Keep pages light enough to open on mobile data'],
  notes = 'THE EDITOR BUILDS A PAGE OUT OF BLOCKS — a paragraph, a heading, an image, a button, a set of columns. Every block can be moved, duplicated and styled on its own. Once that clicks, the interface stops being confusing.

THE FEW THAT DO NEARLY EVERYTHING: paragraph, heading, image, button, columns, group, list, and a spacer. You can build a complete business site with those eight and never open the rest.

HEADINGS ARE STRUCTURE, NOT SIZE. One main heading per page, then sub-headings under it in order. Choosing a heading because it looks big is the commonest mistake; it confuses screen readers, weakens your search ranking, and produces pages that are hard to scan. If you want big text, style it rather than promoting it.

BUILD IT ONCE AND REUSE IT. Save a block arrangement you will want again — a contact strip, a testimonial layout — as a reusable or synced block, and every page that uses it updates together. This is the difference between an hour a page and ten minutes.

NOW THE PART THAT MATTERS MOST HERE, AND THAT DESKTOP-BUILT SITES CONSISTENTLY GET WRONG: MOST OF YOUR VISITORS ARE ON A PHONE, ON MOBILE DATA, PROBABLY NOT A NEW ONE. Check the mobile preview after every section, not at the end. Four columns that look elegant on a laptop become four unreadable slivers or an endless stack on a phone, and the fix is to design the phone version first and let the desktop be the variation.

IMAGES ARE WHERE PAGES GO WRONG AND IT IS ENTIRELY PREVENTABLE. A photograph straight from a phone camera can be several megabytes; ten of them make a page that takes a long time to open and costs the visitor real money in data. RESIZE BEFORE UPLOADING — a width of around 1,500 pixels is plenty for a full-width image — and let a compression plugin handle the rest. A page that opens in two seconds on mobile data will out-perform a prettier one that takes fifteen, because the visitor will still be there.

WRITE ALT TEXT ON EVERY MEANINGFUL IMAGE. It describes the image to somebody using a screen reader, it appears when the image fails to load on a poor connection, and it helps search engines. Three reasons, five seconds.

AND DO NOT FIGHT THE THEME. If you are wrestling a layout into place with spacers and custom widths, the theme is wrong for this design. Changing theme is quick; fighting one costs days and produces something fragile.',
  practice_activity = 'Build one complete page using only the eight core blocks, checking the mobile preview after every section rather than at the end. Before uploading any image, resize it to about 1,500 pixels wide and note the file size before and after. Add alt text to each one. Then save one section you will reuse as a synced block, and finally open the finished page on a real phone using mobile data rather than wifi, and time how long it takes to appear.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why should headings be chosen by structure rather than by size?", "options": ["Headings carry structure for screen readers and search ranking — style text if you only want it bigger", "Larger headings slow the page down", "The theme overrides heading sizes anyway", "Search engines ignore styled text"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What should you do with photographs before uploading them?", "options": ["Resize to around 1,500 pixels wide, because phone photos are several megabytes and cost visitors real data", "Upload at full resolution and let WordPress handle it", "Convert them all to black and white to reduce size", "Upload them at the exact pixel size of the theme''s container"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'e22920da-e663-4a76-bdea-90eaeddba870';

-- Installing Plugins
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Judge whether a plugin is safe before installing it', 'Keep the number of plugins small and deliberate', 'Update and back up in an order that does not break live sites'],
  notes = 'A PLUGIN IS SOMEBODY ELSE''S CODE RUNNING ON YOUR SITE WITH FULL ACCESS TO IT. That sentence is the whole lesson. Plugins are what make WordPress powerful and they are also the most common way a site is broken or compromised.

CHECK FOUR THINGS BEFORE INSTALLING ANYTHING. WHEN WAS IT LAST UPDATED — more than a year ago is a warning, because the web moves and abandoned code becomes a hole. HOW MANY ACTIVE INSTALLATIONS — large numbers mean problems get found and fixed by other people. WHAT THE RECENT REVIEWS SAY, particularly the one-star ones, which describe the failure modes. And DOES THE DEVELOPER ANSWER the support forum. Thirty seconds of this prevents most plugin disasters.

FEWER IS GENUINELY BETTER. Every plugin adds weight, another thing to update, and another possible conflict. Twenty plugins on a small business site is a maintenance burden and a slow page. If a plugin does one small thing you could do another way, do it another way.

THE SHORT LIST MOST SITES ACTUALLY NEED: a security plugin, a backup plugin, an SEO plugin, a caching plugin for speed, a form plugin, and an image compression plugin. That is a complete stack for a business site.

NOW THE WARNING THAT MATTERS MOST, AND IT IS COMMON HERE BECAUSE PREMIUM PLUGINS ARE EXPENSIVE IN SHILLINGS. NEVER INSTALL A CRACKED, NULLED OR FREE-DOWNLOAD VERSION OF A PAID PLUGIN OR THEME. They are one of the most reliable ways to get a site compromised: the modification that removes the licence check is a natural place to hide code that creates hidden administrator accounts, injects spam links, or quietly serves malware to your visitors. You will not see it, the client will not see it, and it may be months before a search engine flags the site as dangerous — by which time the business has lost its reputation and its ranking. If a client cannot afford a premium plugin, use the free alternative, or build the feature more simply, or tell them the honest price. Putting an infected plugin on a client''s site is the single fastest way to end a freelance career, and the money saved is never worth it.

UPDATE IN THE RIGHT ORDER: back up first, then update, then check the site actually still works — the front page, a form, and the checkout if there is one. Automatic updates are reasonable for security releases and risky for major versions of anything the site depends on.

AND DELETE WHAT YOU ARE NOT USING. A deactivated plugin still sits on the server and can still be a vulnerability. Deactivate, confirm nothing broke, then delete.',
  practice_activity = 'Take any plugin you are considering and run the four checks in writing: last updated, active installations, recent one-star reviews, and whether the developer replies in the support forum. Then audit an existing site — your own or a practice one — listing every plugin and deciding for each whether it is essential, replaceable or removable. Deactivate one you do not need, confirm nothing broke, and delete it properly rather than leaving it dormant.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why must you never install a nulled or cracked premium plugin?", "options": ["The modification that removes the licence check is a natural place to hide code that creates hidden admin accounts or serves malware", "It violates the plugin''s terms and the site may be delisted", "They do not receive updates", "They usually do not work properly"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What is the correct order when updating plugins on a live site?", "options": ["Back up first, then update, then check the front page, a form and the checkout still work", "Update everything at once, then back up the working result", "Update one plugin a day to limit risk", "Deactivate all plugins, update, then reactivate"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f9264979-2b87-499a-8f74-c29e6b9006c9';

-- Publishing a Live Business Website
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build the pages a small business actually needs and no more', 'Put what a local visitor wants within one screen', 'Check the site properly on a real phone before handing it over'],
  notes = 'THIS IS THE APPLIED PROJECT: a real site, live, for a real kind of business. Everything before now was preparation.

FIVE PAGES COVER ALMOST EVERY SMALL BUSINESS. Home, which says what you do and for whom. Services or products, with prices or at least price ranges. About, with a real photograph of real people. Contact, with every way to reach you. And whatever one page is specific to the business — a menu, a gallery, a booking form. Six pages nobody maintains is worse than four that are correct.

WHAT A VISITOR HERE WANTS IN THE FIRST SCREEN, before scrolling, and this is where most sites fail: WHAT you sell, WHERE you are, HOW MUCH it costs, and a TAP-TO-CALL or WhatsApp button that works. A phone number that has to be copied out and pasted is friction; a link that opens a chat is not. Put these above everything clever.

PRICES, OR AT LEAST RANGES. The commonest complaint about small business sites is that they hide the price, and the commonest result is that the visitor leaves and calls somebody who published one.

GOOGLE BUSINESS PROFILE MATTERS MORE THAN THE WEBSITE FOR MANY LOCAL BUSINESSES, and it is free. Name, category, location on the map, hours, photographs, and a link to the site. For a business customers find by searching for something near them, this is the first thing to complete and the website supports it rather than replacing it.

BEFORE YOU CALL IT DONE, run the checks that catch what desktop testing hides. HTTPS working, with the padlock, on every page. Every link and every form actually tried, with a test submission that you confirm arrives. Spelling checked by somebody else. A sensible page title for every page, because that is what shows in search results. And a favicon, which takes a minute and makes the site look finished.

THEN TEST IT PROPERLY: ON A REAL PHONE, ON MOBILE DATA, NOT WIFI. That single test is the difference between a site that works for your customers and one that only works in your house. Time how long the home page takes to appear. If it is slow, the cause is almost always images that were never resized.

AND HAND IT OVER PROPERLY. The client gets their logins, written in a document, with a short page of instructions for the three things they will actually want to change: text on a page, a photograph, and the opening hours. A site the owner cannot edit becomes stale within months, and a stale site is worse than none.',
  practice_activity = 'Build and publish one complete five-page site for a real or realistic local business. Put what you sell, where you are, the price and a working WhatsApp or tap-to-call button in the first screen. Complete a Google Business Profile for it as well. Then run the full check: padlock on every page, every form submitted and confirmed as received, spelling read by somebody else, page titles written, favicon added. Finally open it on somebody else''s phone on mobile data and time the home page.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What belongs in the first screen for a local business site?", "options": ["What you sell, where you are, how much it costs, and a working tap-to-call or WhatsApp button", "A large hero image and the company slogan", "The about story and team photographs", "A newsletter signup and social media links"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "How should the site be tested before handover?", "options": ["On a real phone using mobile data rather than wifi, timing how long the home page takes", "In several desktop browsers at different window sizes", "Using the theme''s built-in mobile preview", "By asking the client to check it on their own device"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '7442a0d6-c17f-4e48-9f08-b2a91247426c';

-- Turning It Into Freelance Income
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Price a site as a project, with a deposit and a written scope', 'Sell maintenance, which is where the steady money is', 'Hand over ownership properly and find the next client'],
  notes = 'BUILDING SITES IS PAID WORK AVAILABLE ALMOST IMMEDIATELY AFTER THIS COURSE, and the difference between people who earn from it and people who do not is almost entirely in how they run the business rather than how well they use WordPress.

PRICE PER PROJECT, NOT PER HOUR. Hourly pricing punishes you for getting faster and invites arguments about your speed. A fixed price for a defined site is easier for the client to approve and better for you.

WRITE THE SCOPE DOWN AND KEEP IT SHORT: how many pages, how many rounds of changes, who supplies text and photographs, what the deadline depends on, and what costs extra. THE TEXT AND PHOTOGRAPHS ARE THE THING THAT DELAYS EVERY PROJECT — put in writing that the clock pauses while you wait for them, because you will wait.

TAKE A DEPOSIT BEFORE STARTING. Half is normal. Somebody unwilling to commit anything is unlikely to pay at the end, and this single rule removes most of the bad debt in this trade.

NOW THE PART THAT TURNS A SERIES OF JOBS INTO A BUSINESS: SELL MAINTENANCE. Updates, backups, security checks, small changes, and being the person who answers when something breaks. A modest monthly fee from fifteen clients is a stable income that does not require winning a new project every month, and it is far more valuable than the build fee. Offer it as part of the original proposal rather than as an afterthought; clients who decline it usually come back after the first thing goes wrong.

HOSTING AND DOMAIN GO IN THE CLIENT''S NAME, ON THE CLIENT''S ACCOUNT, PAID BY THE CLIENT. Do not put client sites on your own hosting account as a convenience. When the relationship ends, or when you are unreachable, or when your own card fails, their business goes down with it. Being scrupulous about this is both the right thing and a competitive advantage you can state out loud.

CHARGE FOR THE THINGS BEGINNERS GIVE AWAY: copywriting, photograph sourcing and editing, migrating an existing site, and training the owner. Each is real work and each is separately valuable.

WHERE THE CLIENTS ARE, practically. Businesses you already deal with. Referrals from the first one, asked for directly. Local associations and saccos. And the most reliable route of all — walk into businesses whose current site is broken, slow or non-existent, with a specific observation about it, exactly as the sales course describes. A short, specific, in-person approach converts far better here than any advertisement.

AND BUILD THE PORTFOLIO FROM REAL WORK, even if the first one or two are done at a low price for somebody you know. Three real sites you can point at, with the owners willing to vouch for you, is worth more than any certificate.',
  practice_activity = 'Write your complete offer on one page: a fixed price for a five-page site, what is included, how many rounds of changes, who supplies text and photographs, the deposit, and the monthly maintenance fee. Then pick three local businesses whose sites are broken, slow or missing, note one specific thing about each, and approach one of them in person this week. Finally write the sentence you will use to explain that the domain and hosting will be in their name and paid by them.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What turns a series of website jobs into a stable business?", "options": ["Selling maintenance — a modest monthly fee from many clients does not require winning a new project every month", "Raising the project price each year", "Taking on larger sites", "Hosting client sites on your own account"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Whose account should client hosting and domains sit on?", "options": ["The client''s, paid by the client — otherwise their business goes down with your account or relationship", "Yours, so you can manage updates efficiently", "Yours during the build, transferred at handover", "Either, provided it is written into the contract"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'fd21e846-799c-406f-9ea4-a588f498cc9f';
